nate loop_control_test.rs:486?edge_a_break_stops_the_loop_before_the_iteration_cap redblue_test.rs:4?test_hello_world redblue_test.rs:10?test_numbers redblue_test.rs:22?test_math redblue_test.rs:35?test_list redblue_test.rs:46?test_function stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:242?edge_the_bytecode_vm_answers_the_same_builtins_the_walker_does time_builtin_test.rs:273?time_sleep_of_zero_seconds_is_accepted unless_test.rs:224?edge_unless_as_the_last_statement_in_a_file vacuous tests: 18  $ python3 phases/phase-088/scan.py tests scanned 71 files in tests vacuous tests: 0 ```  **What counts as a comparison, and what does not** — this is the rule the scanner states, and the one its own test file pins:  * A comparison is a place where the program's answer is held against something   else: an `assert_eq!`, `assert_ne!` or `assert_matches!`; an `assert!` whose   condition says more than `is_ok()`; an `==` or `!=`; or a `match` on the   program's own answer against a literal, such as `Ok(Value::YesNo(true))`. * The comparison only counts if what it holds against something else **is** the   program's answer: the output it printed (`take_output`, `.output`, `stdout`),   the error it refused with (`expect_err`), the value it ended on   (`Ok(Value::…)`), or a name bound to one of those. A test that reads the   program's output and never compares it is vacuous, and so is a test whose only   `assert_eq!` compares something that never came from the program. * A name carries the answer forward in four shapes, and all four are followed: it   is **bound** to the answer (`let lines = vm.take_output();`), bound to a name that   already carries it, **put into** a collection the answer is read back out of, or   it is the object the program was **run through** (`harness.run_source(..)`). * Each function is judged **on its own**. A name bound in one function says   nothing about a comparison in another, and a scan that concatenates two   functions' text lets the first function's names satisfy the second function's   comparison — a false "not vacuous" of exactly the kind this scan exists to   remove. * `panic!` does **not** count on its own, because a test that panics only on `Err`   proves the program ran and nothing else; a panic that guards a comparison counts. * A Redblue-level `expect ... to be` **does** count, because the program checks its   own answer and a failed check is a `Runtime` error the run hands back   (`src/interpreter.rs:2331`). * `assert!(run_source(src).is_ok())` does **not** count: it says "the program ran",   which is the shape this scan is looking for. A condition that also names a value   (`is_err()`, `==`, `contains`, `matches`) counts, so a test that asserts a   *failure* was produced is never mistaken for a vacuous one.  The Redblue half of the suite is covered by a different, existing gate: `tests/redblue_suite_test.rs:134` (`every_test_block_carries_an_assertion`) requires **every** `test` block in `tests/*.rb` to carry an `expect` or an `assert.`, and `tests/redblue_suite_test.rs:207` (`edge_suite_asserts_that_failures_are_produced`) requires the Redblue suite to assert that failures are produced. The scanner is therefore not extended to `.rb` files: that half of the suite already has its own per-block gate, and a second scanner over the same files would be the same assertion twice.  ## The mutation experiments  A test that cannot fail is a smoke test, so both experiments are run against the **new** tests, with the implementation changed and then restored.  **Round 1–2 — a builtin that answers wrongly instead of refusing.** `src/stdlib.rs:580`, `sqrt`'s text arm changed from `_ => None` (refuse) to `_ => Some(Value::Number(0.0))` (answer wrongly):  ``` ---- edge_a_builtin_that_cannot_answer_is_refused_by_the_library_not_answered_wrongly stdout ---- `say sqrt("nine")` must be refused, not answered: ()  ---- edge_a_builtin_given_a_text_says_nothing_and_names_the_refusal stdout ---- `say sqrt("nine")` must be refused by the walker: Nothing ```  Two tests went red, including the two this phase added. Under the old `Ok(_) => {}` body the same mutation returned `Ok` and passed — that is the defect, demonstrated rather than asserted.  **This round — a builtin that answers the right characters in the wrong *ki [... truncated: 15000 of 32196 bytes shown for previous REPORT.md for phase-088 — full tree available in the clone ...]  ```  ### HUMAN RETRY NOTE — read this before the old REPORT below  A human ordered this retry and wrote why. When this note, your PROMPT.md and the old REPORT below disagree, this note and your PROMPT.md outrank the old report.``` # RETRY-NOTE — phase-088  **This outranks the previous REPORT below.** Four attempts have now failed this phase for exactly one reason, and it is not the code.  ## What the gate says, verbatim  ``` PASS test quota met: 8 rust / 0 redblue (need 3 or 2) PASS edge case test present: 8 FAIL no test asserts a failure is produced - mandatory      (Rust: is_err()/should_panic; Redblue: try ... catch error) ```  **That is the whole failure.** One check. Everything else is green: 1774 tests passing, 0 failing, fmt clean, clippy clean.  ## Why your previous attempts did not fix it  Your REPORT says "cargo test --all-targets 1774/0/0" and that is **true**. The mistake is reading that as "the gate passes".  It does not, and it is worth being precise about why. The failing check is not asking whether your tests are **correct**. It is asking whether any test asserts that something **fails**. Those are different properties:  - 1774 passing tests prove your code does the right thing when things go right. - The mandatory check wants proof that your code does the **right thing when   something goes wrong** — that a bad input produces a clean, catchable error   rather than a panic, a silent wrong value, or a crash.  A suite can be entirely green and still satisfy nothing here. That is why attempt after attempt passed 1774 tests and still failed.  ## The fix — one test